NAME
    spawn - launch one owner-scoped child application

SYNOPSIS
    spawn [--capture|--status] [--env NAME=VALUE]... COMMAND [ARG...]

DESCRIPTION
    Launches COMMAND as an owner-scoped child of spawn itself, waits for it,
    and returns its exit status. The child is admitted through the process
    launch service, so its package capabilities must attenuate spawn's own
    manifest; a child cannot gain authority its launcher does not hold.

    COMMAND resolves exactly as it does for the shell. A bare name selects
    /bin/COMMAND.kex, and a name containing / selects that exact package path
    relative to the current directory. No suffix, PATH search, or implicit
    current-directory lookup is applied.

    The child inherits spawn's immutable environment with PWD set to its launch
    directory. It does not inherit the session terminal: standard input is an
    empty stream that reports end of input immediately. Standard output and
    error are inherited unless --capture redirects output through a pipe.

OPTIONS
    --capture      Read the child's standard output through a bounded pipe and
                   write it after the child becomes terminal.
    --status       Report the child's exit status on standard error as
                   spawn-status: N.
    --env NAME=VALUE
                   Supply one entry to the child. An entry replaces the
                   inherited value of the same name and is otherwise appended.
                   NAME must be a nonempty ASCII alphanumeric or underscore
                   identifier that does not start with a digit. Repeating one
                   name across two --env options is rejected, because the
                   launch environment admits exactly one value per name.

    Options may appear in any order but must precede COMMAND. The first
    argument that is not a recognized option starts the child invocation, so
    the child receives its own options unchanged.

LIMITS
    A child environment holds at most 128 entries and 2,048 aggregate UTF-8
    bytes, including inherited entries. Exceeding either limit, or naming one
    variable twice, fails before any child is created.

EXAMPLES
    spawn --status echo child-ready
    spawn --capture ls /bin
    spawn --env LUA_PATH_5_5=/share/lua/?.lua lua -e 'print(package.path)'

SEE ALSO
    ps, sh
